ng to be  coming in the budget it's going to be a  bit of a longer project  well the Prime Minister referred to the  spending review next year because in  terms of public services that is when we  will set out our plans for spending over  the next few years in the normal way  what I will do in the budget tomorrow is  set out  broadly a spending envelope going  forward but the detail will be for the  spending review because that's when we  quite properly look at the envelope of  money available and decide collectively  what our priorities are for the next few  years that to me is the responsible way  to budget first of all you work out how  much money you've got and then you  decide what your priorities are for  spending it and the problem you've got  isn't it that people aren't going to  feel like austerity is actually ending  unless they can see it having an impact  in their lives and things have been  difficult I've used as you've said you  know since 2010 school budgets down  eight percent twenty thousand fewer  police in England and Wales the welfare  Secretary's admitted that universal  credit will leave some people worse off  these people aren't going to believe you  when you say there's light at the end of  the tunnel unless you address some of  these things well school spending is  protected in real terms party engine  sixteen police funding we protected in  the 2015 spending review settlement but  I accept that public services have been  under pressure they've made enormous  efforts to deliver efficiency doing more  with less and people do want to know  that we've reached a turning point where  we can provide more support for our  public services but don't don't forget  we've already made and I can I can talk  about this because it's not a secret in  the budget we've already made the most  important budget announcement back in  June that we're going to put 80 plus  billion pounds over the next five years  into the NHS so over 20 billion pounds a  year of additional real terms money by  the end of this forecast period  transformational investment I noticed  said that you talked about the NHS you  talked about schools who talked up the  police you didn't talk about universal  credit and actually for some of those  people who are going to be worse off the  most vulnerable people in society many  of them they're not going to feel like  austerity is ending according to the  resolution foundation three million  working families losing 2,500 pounds a  year a 50% rise in people using food  banks in areas where Universal Credit  has been rolled out I mean you know  putting the covert accountants sort of  owns a society do you understand what  it's like for those people so univer  Universal Credit is a very important  innovation the legacy benefit system  that we operated for years left millions  of people stranded out of work unable to  work they'd have made themselves worse  off by going into work what Universal  Credit does is ensure that everybody is  supported to move into work and that  they are always better off in work so  it's a very important structural reform  but if you are a parent it was going to  a food that because you can't work out  how to give food on the table so you can  understand why that might feel like the  kind of back I can't exactly so let me  finish the point so I think that  Universal Credit is a very important  innovation and once it's rolled out  people will see that it's a much better  way to run a welfare system but there  have been issues around the transition  from legacy benefits into Universal  Credit many of the kind of problems that  you're talking about occur when people  move from one system into the other and  there's a mismatch in the way the  systems work I've already put over two  billion pounds into over the last two  budgets into smoothing that transition  you know we continue well we continue to  look at how this process is working and  if if we find cliff edges and  difficulties frictions in in the move  from the old benefit system to Universal  Credit then of course we'll always try  to smooth those out and and be pragmatic  about this and now I know that you're  looking at investing in roads for lots  of people getting potholes on their  Street or a very visible sign of  austerity so what are you planning on  doing well I am going to make some more  additional money available for local  authorities to deal with urgent repairs  potholes in roads repair work on bridges  these are small projects in themselves  but they make a huge difference to  people and actually they make a huge  difference to the efficiency of the road  system overall so this is money well  spent  well in  but more widely we have to look at  investment in our roads and I will be  making an important announcement about  the hypothecation of vehicle excise duty  so that it is all spent on funding our  strategic roads Network for the future  that's an important investment in  Britain's productivity I'm also  interested in your plans for the high  string and  debenhams announcing that 50 stores are  closing just the latest retailer to  really come under under pressure and I  kind of help think sometimes that we  talk a lot about subsidizing some  industries you know the steel industry  the banks but for lots of people the  high street is absolutely at the heart  of their community so do you recognize  that any penny won't do anything about  it so I absolutely recognize that the  high street plays a critical role in  many communities but we have to  recognize as well that the change that's  driving the the the evolution of our  high street can't be put back in the  bottle the British have taken to online  shopping like no other nation on earth  and for better or for worse we have to  recognize that that is happening so our  high streets have to change we want to  protect them because we want them to  remain at the heart of our communities  but we can't do that by freezing the  model of 10 or 20 years ago in a spec it  won't work so we've got to help the high  street to evolve and the high street of  the future frankly will have fewer  retail outlets and more leisure  destinations more food and drink outlets  I expect that high streets of the future  will be smaller with more of the  peripheral areas around them redeveloped  as how seems like business rates or  online transaction taxes yeah of course  we'll look at the ways in which we can  help to smooth this process but it's  very important that we recognize that  the high street will have to change ID  jinkin we can support the high street as  it changes but we can't prevent that  change from happen where do you shop  Guildford usually which has a actually a  very nice high street but are you an  online shop as well do you use I am an  online shopper I do yeah yeah yes and  now I'm also keen of course talk about  breakfast because this is really you  know the back  drop for your budget and you've been  pretty only honest about some of the  consequences of not getting a deal and  ten percent for example can come from  GDP if that happens so if there isn't a  deal will you still be unable to end or  starting well I'm very confident that we  will negotiate a deal with the European  Union and the main element of that deal  has to be that we continue to have  frictionless access to European markets  or reciprocal it if you don't get what  you want will you be able to end  austerity if there isn't if we don't get  a deal if we were to leave the European  Union without any deal I think that's an  extremely unlikely situation but of  course we have to prepare and plan for  all eventualities as any prudent  government would if we were to find  ourselves in that situation then we  would need to take a different approach  to the future of Britain's economy we  would need to look at a different  strategy and frankly we'd need to have a  new budget that set out a different  strategy for the future unless an D the  the the approach that I'm going to be  setting out tomorrow is based on the  assumption as is the OB R's report of a  a deal being done with the European  Union and us continuing to be able to  trade on reasonable terms with our  closest neighbors in Europe so I'm just  - just to really try and get to the gist  of what you're saying if there isn't a  deal which I know you don't want and  would there be less money for government  departments what austerity continue well  there would be certain we would have to  wait and see what the situation was if  we if we left the European Union without  a deal we would want to see how markets  and businesses and consumers responded  to that and then as any responsible  government would we would take  appropriate fiscal measures to protect  the economy to prepare us for the future  and to strike out in a new direction  that would ensure that Britain was able  to succeed whatever the circumstances we  found ourselves in and of course it  wouldn't only be us as the government it  would also be the Bank of England  responsible for monetary policy that  would be looking at that situation and  taking action to  protect the economy even if we get a  deal or we can be worse off after  braixen  my job is to make sure that we're better  off and we go on getting better off  we've had you know for 200 years with a  few with a few intervening periods we've  had 200 years in which our living  standards have consistently raised over  my lifetime people's living standards  have almost doubled we want to go on  seeing that growth in living standards  so that we can make good on our promise  to the next generation that they too  will be better off than their parents  okay and now some people in your party  have been a bit scathing about your  attitude towards brexit and Boris  Johnson describing the Treasury is the  enemy of remain and you've also been  described as eel I mean are you a bit  gloomy about breakfast is that fair  no I don't think so at all I think what  we've done is the Treasury has done as a  department at the center of government  has taken the decision of the British  people and sought to deliver it  implement it in a way that protects our  economy protects living standards and  delivers on all the many other things  that the British people want because yes  people went out in their millions to  express an opinion on this very  important issue  but if you go and ask them whether this  is the most important issue to them many  of them will tell you no I'm concerned  about policing about education about the  health service about my own of course  and that's what I'm saying our job is to  take the instruction of the British  people on brexit and to ensure that we  deliver it in a way that also allows us  to go on delivering what they want to  see on health services on education on  police on defence on the environment and  all the other things that they regard as  very important to them and just finally  do you think you're able to be perhaps a  little bit more honest when it comes to  brexit and some of your cabinet  colleagues because you're perhaps the  only person sitting around the table who  doesn't fancy the job as Prime Minister  well I hope I'm always honest I always  try to be honest sometimes gets me into  a bit of trouble from some of my  advisers but I always try to be honest I  always try to answer the  that I'm asked because actually I think  one of the ways in which we rebuild  faith and confidence in politics is by  politicians trying to be honest with  people about the really big challenges  that we face do you think some of your  other party members should be a bit more  honest  well I'm sure all my colleagues are very  honest with you all the time Sophie yes  and I'm sure they'd all be delighted to  come and sit in this chair and be  equally honest and open with you and  Chancellor thank you very much thank you

